    "name": "ELK-BLEDOM BLE LED Strip Controller",
    "notes": "Generic BLE LED strip controllers sold under dozens of brand names: JACKYLED, auraLED, HueLite, MELK, LEDBLE, ELK-BULB, ELK-LAMPL, and many others. Extremely cheap ($2-15) PWM LED controllers with a common BLE protocol. Advertises HID service 0x1812 but does not actually implement it — ignore.\nPROTOCOL: fixed 9-byte packets with envelope [0x7E, seq, cmd, p1, p2, p3, p4, flag, 0xEF]. Byte 2 is a per-command sequence number (0x00 works; app uses unique values). Byte 8 is typically 0x00 or 0x10 but does not affect functionality. No bonding, no auth. NOT write-only: devices send 9-byte 7E…EF state notifications on characteristic 0xFFF4 (power state 0x23/0xF0/0x01 = on, 0x24/0x00 = off, plus RGB and brightness), and a state query command exists (7e 00 01 fa 00 00 00 00 ef).\nCommands are grouped: command byte 3 identifies the operation category, byte 4 the sub-command. The app distinguishes \"data\" vs \"control\" messages but they use the same packet format.\nDISCOVERY: service UUID 0xFFF0 is the reliable signal. Advertised names vary widely: \"ELK-BLEDOM\", \"MELK\", \"LEDBLE\", \"ELK-BULB\", \"ELK-LAMPL\". No pairing required — any central in range can connect and write.\nUUID FAMILIES: the 0xFFF0/0xFFF3/0xFFF4 set does not cover every advertised name. LEDBLE, JACKYLED, \"LED-\" and XROCKER variants use write 0xFFE1 / notify 0xFFE2; DMRRBA-007 uses the Nordic UART service (write 0x6E400002, notify 0x6E400003).\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH for service UUID, characteristic UUID, packet format, color/brightness/color-temperature commands — sourced from github.com/FergusInLondon/ELK-BLEDOM, a clean-room RE from the Android APK. State readback (0xFFF4), the MELK/MODELX login handshake, the alternate UUID families and the scheduler/time-sync/mic commands are sourced from github.com/dave-code-ruiz/elkbledom (actively maintained HACS component). Commands beyond the visual ones (timing, mic streaming) are documented but less tested.\n",
    "protocol": "ble",
